        "response_text": "A canary token is a harmless, trackable artifact planted in a system to alert defenders when it gets accessed, moved, or used unexpectedly.\n\nCommon examples include:\n- A fake API key or credential\n- A document with an embedded callback\n- A URL, DNS name, or email address that should never be touched in normal operation\n\nIf an attacker, malware sample, insider, or misconfigured tool interacts with it, the token “phones home” and gives a signal that something suspicious happened. In security testing and monitoring, this helps teams detect unauthorized access early, trace data exposure paths, and validate that detection pipelines are working.\n\nThey are useful because they are low-noise by design: since no legitimate workflow should use them, any interaction is worth investigating.",
